<%# The swappable core of the session view. Every form posts with htmx targeting
    #session-body, so a tap re-renders progress, the set row, and the RPE state
    together; without JS the same forms post normally and the route redirects. %>
<%# One segment per lift, not per set. A set is the wrong unit for a bar: five movements
    of six sets drew thirty dashes twelve pixels wide on a phone, which is a barcode, and
    it disagreed with the panel directly beneath it saying "1 of 5" over five dots. The
    bar now counts what the session is made of, which is what #176 asks for.

    A segment fills with its own sets rather than flipping whole, so a lift four sets into
    six is two thirds lime and reads as underway -- the distinction an all-or-nothing
    segment throws away, and the one that matters most while you are in the middle of a
    lift. The slices are the sets themselves, laid out with no gap inside a rounded,
    clipped track, so they read as one bar filling rather than as six dashes. That keeps
    every set the old bar showed and needs no width computed into a style attribute or an
    arbitrary class -- either of which would have to survive htmx swapping this partial
    out from under the CDN's JIT on every tap. %>
<%# data-lift-segment marks each segment with the lift it belongs to, so the script at the
    foot of session.erb can say which one you are looking at. The bar answers "how much of
    this session is done"; on its own it could not answer "and where am I in it", which is
    the question a swipe asks -- the bar sat perfectly still while the screen moved, which
    is #212. Marking the current segment makes one bar answer both.

    Which panel is centred is a scroll position, and CSS cannot see one, so this is set from
    JS. It degrades to the bar exactly as it was: without the script every segment is
    unmarked, which is what it looked like before, and the per-panel dots and "3 of 5" are
    rendered server-side and go on saying where you are regardless. %>
<div class="sticky top-0 bg-gray-100 py-2">
  <div class="flex gap-0.5" aria-label="Session progress">
    <%- session_lifts.each_with_index do |lift, position| %>
      <div data-lift-segment="<%= position %>" class="flex h-2 flex-1 overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-gray-300">
        <%- lift.each do |set| %>
          <div class="flex-1 <%= set[:is_completed] ? 'bg-lime-500' : '' %>"></div>
        <%- end %>
      </div>
    <%- end %>
  </div>
  <%# Sets rather than lifts, and deliberately: the bar is now the answer to "how many
      movements", so the line under it is where the exact set count still has to live. %>
  <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-500">
    <%= @sets.count { |set| set[:is_completed] } %> of <%= @sets.length %> sets
  </p>
</div>

<%# One lift a screen, swiped between, rather than five lifts down one scroll. Five lifts
    and twenty sets is a page nobody can find their place in: the lift you are on is
    somewhere past the ones you are not.

    Scroll snap and no JavaScript. A flex row that overflows is already a real scroller --
    momentum, rubber-banding and all -- on every phone, and snap-mandatory makes it stop on
    a lift rather than between two. A gesture library would have to be re-bound every time
    htmx swaps this partial out, and would be a worse swipe than the one the platform
    ships. overscroll-x-contain is the one part that is not obvious: without it a swipe
    that runs off the last lift is handed to Safari, which reads it as the back gesture and
    leaves the session.

    Restored to a stack from 640px up, and that is a decision rather than timidity. A swipe
    is a thumb; a mouse has no horizontal wheel, and the overlay scrollbar that would be the
    only other way across only appears once you are already scrolling, so on a desktop these
    panels would be every lift but the first sitting where a mouse cannot reach them. The
    column is max-w-md at every width -- a desktop was never shown five lifts side by side,
    it was shown the same narrow column -- so the stack is what a desktop loses nothing by
    keeping.

    The panels stay in source order and every one of them is full of buttons, so Tab still
    walks the session lift by lift and the browser scrolls each panel in as it goes -- a
    scroll container whose contents are all focusable needs no tabindex of its own. %>
<div id="lift-panels" class="flex snap-x snap-mandatory overflow-x-auto overscroll-x-contain sm:block sm:overflow-x-visible">
<%- session_lifts.each_with_index do |lift, position| %>
  <%# 88% rather than the full width, so an edge of the next lift is always showing. That
      is #206, and it is the answer both bits of research give.

      Nielsen Norman on mobile carousels: "The illusion of continuity, created by half
      images or text that look like they are continued beyond the vertical edge of the
      screen, is a strong carousel cue", and against what this screen was relying on --
      "Dots are generally weak signifiers: because they are small, people often don't
      notice them." Smashing Magazine puts the same thing as a rule: "Always indicate a
      slice of the upcoming slide", and "Never use progress dots as the only way for users
      to navigate the carousel."

      The dots and the "3 of 5" stay. They say how many there are and where you sit, which
      a cut edge cannot; what they were failing at was being the only thing saying a swipe
      exists at all. Same for the progress bar #212 taught to move -- these are three cues
      doing different jobs, and only this one is visible before you touch anything.

      Restored to the full width from 640px up along with everything else: the lifts are a
      stack there, and 88% of a column would just be a narrow column. %>
  <section class="mt-6 w-[88%] shrink-0 snap-center px-1 sm:w-full sm:px-0" aria-labelledby="lift-<%= position + 1 %>">
    <h2 id="lift-<%= position + 1 %>" class="text-xl text-gray-900">
      <%= @exercises[lift.first[:exercise_id]]&.name %>
    </h2>
    <%# A swipe surface nobody knows is there is worse than the scroll it replaced, so each
        panel says where it sits. The dots say there are others and roughly where; the count
        says how many, which dots alone never do. Both are written per panel and are true as
        rendered, which is why neither needs a scroll listener to stay honest.

        Not on a session of one lift, where there is nowhere to swipe to and "1 of 1" is a
        line of furniture. Not from 640px up either, where the lifts are stacked and every
        one of them is visibly on the page already. %>
    <%- if session_lifts.length > 1 %>
      <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-2 sm:hidden">
        <div class="flex gap-1" aria-hidden="true">
          <%- session_lifts.each_index do |dot| %>
            <div class="h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full <%= dot == position ? 'bg-gray-500' : 'bg-gray-300' %>"></div>
          <%- end %>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500"><%= position + 1 %> of <%= session_lifts.length %></p>
      </div>
    <%- end %>

    <%# Small caps in gray-500 is this screen's label register -- Warmup, RPE and Plate
        math all wear it, so that a label is always recognisable as one and never as a
        value. It was gray-400, which is 2.54:1 on white; a line nobody can read at
        arm's length with a bar in their hands may as well not be on the screen. %>
    <%- unless lift.select { |set| set[:is_warmup] }.empty? %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Warmup</p>
    <%- end %>
    <%# A warmup row and a working-set row are the same row. #207: they were text-lg in a
        rounded-lg with py-2 against text-2xl font-semibold in a rounded-xl with py-3, which
        made a ramp step and a working set read as two different kinds of object when they
        are one kind -- a set, with a load, a rep count and a Done button. Both are now
        text-xl font-medium in the same box, and what says which is which is the "Warmup"
        label above this list, in words, where it was already being said.

        This does not put back the fade #133 removed, and must not: the list carried
        opacity-75, which took the Done button on a warmup to 4.24:1 against 7.56:1 at full
        strength -- a washed-out control, which is what a disabled one looks like, on the
        button that is the first thing anybody taps in a session. Size was what replaced
        that fade; the label is what replaces the size. %>
    <ul>
      <%- lift.select { |set| set[:is_warmup] }.each do |set| %>
        <li class="mt-2 rounded-xl border px-3 py-3 <%= row_style(set) %>">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-900">
                <%= load_label(set) %>
              </span>
              <%- if set[:is_barbell] %>
                <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-gray-500">
                  <span class="uppercase tracking-wide">Plate math</span>
                  <%= plate_label(set) %>
                </span>
              <%- end %>
              <%- if changed_from_plan?(set) %>
                <%# Grey, and not the amber it wore until #214. This line is the whole of how
                    a changed set is now marked -- the row tint went to two states, done and
                    not -- and it says what changed in words, which a colour never did. Grey
                    is what the plate-math line above it uses, and this is the same kind of
                    thing: a fact about the set, read after the load rather than instead.

                    It used to be shown only on a completed set, which was fine while saving
                    a correction completed one. #215 stopped that, so the guard had to go
                    with it: a set corrected to 145 and not yet done is exactly when you most
                    want to see that the sheet said 150. An untouched set cannot reach here
                    anyway -- the generator writes weight equal to planned_weight, so
                    changed_from_plan? is false until something changes it. %>
                <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  planned <%= weight_label(set[:planned_weight]) %> &times; <%= set[:planned_reps] %>
                </div>
              <%- end %>
            </div>
            <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete" method="post"
                  hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete"
                  hx-target="#session-body" hx-swap="innerHTML">
              <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/sets/#{set[:id]}/complete") %>
              <button type="submit"
                      class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 border px-5 py-3 text-base font-semibold
                             <%= complete_style(set) %>">
                <%= set[:is_completed] ? 'Undo' : 'Done' %>
              </button>
            </form>
          </div>

          <%# A warmup used to offer Done and nothing else, so a ramp taken differently could
              not be said. That is not a small gap: the ramp is computed from a percentage of
              the top set, and a lifter who adds a step, starts on a 35 lb bar, or finds 115
              heavy and repeats it leaves the session claiming a ramp nobody lifted. row_style
              and changed_from_plan? were already willing to tint such a row amber -- the
              generator writes planned_weight and planned_reps for warmups exactly as it does
              for working sets -- and there was simply no way to make one.

              The disclosure is the working set's, down to the wording, because a warmup and a
              working set are the same act at different weights and learning two habits for it
              is one too many. Closed it stays as quiet as the old row was. The boxes inside
              are the working set's size too: the rule above is that a warmup is played down
              by being smaller, but it applies to what is read, not to what is typed one
              handed with chalk on.

              No RPE row here, though every working set has one. A warmup is submaximal by
              definition, so its rating is a number nobody will read back, and five buttons at
              48px would cost each of these rows more height than the whole row costs now. %>
          <details class="group mt-1">
            <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center text-xs text-gray-500"><svg class="mr-1.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M7.21 14.77a.75.75 0 0 1 .02-1.06L11.168 10 7.23 6.29a.75.75 0 1 1 1.04-1.08l4.5 4.25a.75.75 0 0 1 0 1.08l-4.5 4.25a.75.75 0 0 1-1.06-.02Z" clip-rule="evenodd" /></svg>Lifted something else</summary>
            <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete" method="post"
                  hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete"
                  hx-target="#session-body" hx-swap="innerHTML" class="mt-1 flex items-end gap-2">
              <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/sets/#{set[:id]}/complete") %>
              <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="weight-<%= set[:id] %>">
                Weight
                <input value="<%= weight_label(set[:weight]) %>" id="weight-<%= set[:id] %>" name="weight" type="number" min="0" step="any" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" class="mt-1 block w-24 rounded-lg border-0 py-2 text-center text-lg text-gray-900 ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-300 focus:ring-2 focus:ring-lime-500">
              </label>
              <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="reps-<%= set[:id] %>">
                Reps
                <input value="<%= set[:reps] %>" id="reps-<%= set[:id] %>" name="reps" type="number" min="0" step="1" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" class="mt-1 block w-20 rounded-lg border-0 py-2 text-center text-lg text-gray-900 ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-300 focus:ring-2 focus:ring-lime-500">
              </label>
              <button type="submit"
                      class="<%= button_style %> border border-sky-800 bg-sky-800 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-sky-900">
                Save
              </button>
            </form>
          </details>
        </li>
      <%- end %>
    </ul>

    <ul class="mt-3">
      <%- lift.reject { |set| set[:is_warmup] }.each do |set| %>
        <li class="mt-2 rounded-xl border px-3 py-3 <%= row_style(set) %>">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-900">
                <%= load_label(set) %>
              </span>
              <%# Same words and same treatment as the warmup rows above: a warmup and a
                  working set are loaded off the same rack, so the line that says how is
                  not allowed to be a different thing in the two places. %>
              <%- if set[:is_barbell] %>
                <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  <span class="uppercase tracking-wide">Plate math</span>
                  <%= plate_label(set) %>
                </div>
              <%- end %>
              <%- if changed_from_plan?(set) %>
                <%# Grey, and not the amber it wore until #214. This line is the whole of how
                    a changed set is now marked -- the row tint went to two states, done and
                    not -- and it says what changed in words, which a colour never did. Grey
                    is what the plate-math line above it uses, and this is the same kind of
                    thing: a fact about the set, read after the load rather than instead.

                    It used to be shown only on a completed set, which was fine while saving
                    a correction completed one. #215 stopped that, so the guard had to go
                    with it: a set corrected to 145 and not yet done is exactly when you most
                    want to see that the sheet said 150. An untouched set cannot reach here
                    anyway -- the generator writes weight equal to planned_weight, so
                    changed_from_plan? is false until something changes it. %>
                <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  planned <%= weight_label(set[:planned_weight]) %> &times; <%= set[:planned_reps] %>
                </div>
              <%- end %>
            </div>
            <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete" method="post"
                  hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete"
                  hx-target="#session-body" hx-swap="innerHTML">
              <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/sets/#{set[:id]}/complete") %>
              <button type="submit"
                      class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 border px-5 py-3 text-base font-semibold
                             <%= complete_style(set) %>">
                <%= set[:is_completed] ? 'Undo' : 'Done' %>
              </button>
            </form>
          </div>

          <%# Rated per set, not just per session: five lifts in a session are five
              different questions, and the answer decays within seconds of racking the
              bar. Submitting a rating completes the set, because rating one is saying
              you lifted it. %>
          <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete" method="post"
                hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete"
                hx-target="#session-body" hx-swap="innerHTML" class="mt-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/sets/#{set[:id]}/complete") %>
            <span class="mr-1 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">RPE</span>
            <%# Rated 45 times in a session, one-handed, with chalk on. flex-1 divides the
                row rather than leaving dead space, so the targets are as wide as the
                screen allows; min-h-12 is 48px, which clears the 44px minimum. %>
            <%- (6..10).each do |rating| %>
              <button value="<%= rating %>" class="<%= rpe_style(set, rating) %> min-h-12 flex-1 rounded-lg border border-gray-300 text-base font-semibold" type="submit" name="rpe">
                <%= rating %>
              </button>
            <%- end %>
          </form>

          <details class="group mt-2">
            <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center text-xs text-gray-500"><svg class="mr-1.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M7.21 14.77a.75.75 0 0 1 .02-1.06L11.168 10 7.23 6.29a.75.75 0 1 1 1.04-1.08l4.5 4.25a.75.75 0 0 1 0 1.08l-4.5 4.25a.75.75 0 0 1-1.06-.02Z" clip-rule="evenodd" /></svg>Lifted something else</summary>
            <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete" method="post"
                  hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete"
                  hx-target="#session-body" hx-swap="innerHTML" class="mt-2 flex items-end gap-2">
              <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/sets/#{set[:id]}/complete") %>
              <%# step="5" here was never a 5 lb grid. HTML counts steps from a step base,
                  which is the min attribute if there is one and otherwise the value
                  attribute -- and value is this row's own weight. So a row sitting at 138
                  accepted 143 and 133 and refused 135, 140 and 145, which is the refusal in
                  the screenshot on #114. A rack with 1 lb plates has an increment of 2, so
                  weights like 138 are what the generator writes for anybody who owns micro
                  plates: the trap fires on ordinary data, not on a corner case.

                  min="0" pins the base at zero so a row's own weight can never define the
                  grid again, and it rules out a negative load while it is there.

                  step is "any" since #141 widened sets.weight to numeric(7, 2). It was 1,
                  and the reason was the column: posting "137.5" against an integer got
                  PG::InvalidTextRepresentation, the set was left exactly as it was, and htmx
                  does not swap a 500 -- so Save appeared to do nothing at all, with no bubble
                  to explain it. Refusing half a pound in the browser, where it cost one
                  message, beat accepting it and losing the whole set. The column holds it
                  now, so the refusal has nothing left to protect.

                  "any" rather than "0.25", because this box records what was lifted rather
                  than what was prescribed. A machine stack is whatever it is, and a lifter
                  who has just moved a pin has no reason to argue with the browser about
                  whether the number is on somebody's grid. What a rack can be loaded to is a
                  different question, asked at generation, and #140 is where it is answered. %>
              <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="weight-<%= set[:id] %>">
                Weight
                <input value="<%= weight_label(set[:weight]) %>" id="weight-<%= set[:id] %>" name="weight" type="number" min="0" step="any" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" class="mt-1 block w-24 rounded-lg border-0 py-2 text-center text-lg text-gray-900 ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-300 focus:ring-2 focus:ring-lime-500">
              </label>
              <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="reps-<%= set[:id] %>">
                Reps
                <input value="<%= set[:reps] %>" id="reps-<%= set[:id] %>" name="reps" type="number" min="0" step="1" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" class="mt-1 block w-20 rounded-lg border-0 py-2 text-center text-lg text-gray-900 ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-300 focus:ring-2 focus:ring-lime-500">
              </label>
              <%# Saving an edit is the same kind of thing as tapping Done -- an action,
                  not a state -- so it wears the same fill. Its hover used to jump to
                  lime-500, which turned a blue button green under the thumb and said the
                  set was done before it was. %>
              <button type="submit"
                      class="<%= button_style %> border border-sky-800 bg-sky-800 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-sky-900">
                Save
              </button>
            </form>
          </details>
        </li>
      <%- end %>
    </ul>

    <%# The scale belongs under the buttons that ask for it, and only under those. A panel
        of nothing but warmups asks for no rating -- the ramp rows carry no RPE row -- so
        it would be a table answering a question that panel never puts. %>
    <%- unless lift.all? { |set| set[:is_warmup] } %>
      <%== render('workouts/_rpe_help') %>
    <%- end %>
  </section>
<%- end %>
</div>

<%# No copy of the rating scale here. The last lift panel's is immediately above this form,
    so a second one put two identical disclosures on screen with nothing between them but
    the five rating buttons, which is what #175 is a picture of.

    Nor is one rendered when no panel carried it, which is a session of nothing but
    warmups. A warmup is submaximal by definition and asks for no rating -- that is why the
    ramp rows have no RPE row and warmup-only panels have no scale -- so a session that is
    all ramp has nothing on it the scale would be explaining. %>
